catalogue` calls `counted_ids()` fresh on each call, so every open of a file starts at UUID(int=1). The default it replaces (`random_id`) never repeats, and PictureStore's docstring states that rule for its token. Today no test reopens a file in a way that mints ids twice into one table: establish_the_wall seeds once, and walls_name_displays carries only while the old columns exist. A test that restores those columns and reopens would hit a primary-key collision, and that would fail loudly rather than silently. Either derive the counter from the path, or note the per-open scope in `open_catalogue`'s docstring. No edit is needed unless such a test appears.", "files": ["arrt/tests/fakes.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "The `uncacheable` marker is registered in conftest.py, but every other marker lives in arrt/pyproject.toml's `markers` list", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. pyproject.toml is where this project declares and explains its markers. The new one is added with `config.addinivalue_line` in `pytest_configure`, and the docstring's reason (a runner registers it itself, a local run does not) applies just as well to a pyproject entry. Consider moving it into the `markers` list with its one-line meaning, so the declared set has one home. It is harmless as it stands: `pytest --markers` shows it either way.", "files": ["arrt/tests/conftest.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Two catalogue openers coexist in the tests, and the cache-safe one is a convention written only in a fakes.py docstring", "recommendation": "Scope: class. About 25 test files, and conftest's `_open_seeded_catalogue`, still call `open_catalogue_file` directly. The record descopes the services' own clocks and ids, which explains the conftest site. A new test copies whichever opener its neighbour uses, and nothing tells the author that one choice keeps the test reusable from the cache and the other does not. docs/testing.md is where the suite's conventions live; it could name `fakes.open_catalogue` and the passed-in clocks as the default. Alternatively, convert the remaining callers as #435 continues. No edit is owed by this branch.", "files": ["arrt/tests/fakes.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "fakes.open_catalogue takes `**given: Any`, which erases open_catalogue_file's keyword signature", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. A misspelled keyword (`wall=` for `wall_name=`) still fails at call time inside open_catalogue_file, so nothing is lost silently. The cost is that type checkers and editors no longer see the opener's parameters. Spelling out `wall_name`, `now` and `new_id` as keyword parameters would keep them visible. Optional.", "files": ["arrt/tests/fakes.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "The test-side cacheability convention lives only in this branch's change record", "recommendation": "The convention is to open catalogues through `fakes.open_catalogue` rather than `open_catalogue_file`, use the `kept`/`kept_clock` fixtures, pass `now=`/`staging_token=`/`clock=` to PictureStore and CatalogueBackup, and decline a test whose child process is its point with `@pytest.mark.uncacheable(why)`. It is recorded in `.prawduct/changes/test-cacheability.md`, which is history, and in scattered docstrings. `docs/testing.md` covers only the browser and live suites. A test author who reaches for `open_catalogue_file` or `KeptAnswers(path)` makes that test uncacheable, and only testament's throttled `cache` advice will say so. No edit is wanted for this merge. If the count is to hold, a short paragraph in `docs/testing.md` (or the curation-plane conftest module docstring) naming these helpers and the marker would give the convention a durable home. This is the class, bounded to helpers that read the clock or randomness through production defaults.", "files": ["arrt/tests/fakes.py", "arrt/tests/conftest.py"], "oid": "O-6"}], "duration_secon